Given below are two statements:&nbsp;
<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td><span style="white-space:nowrap;"><strong>Statement I:</strong></span>&nbsp;</td><td>On reducing the volume of a gas at a constant temperature, the pressure of the gas increases.</td></tr><tr><td><span style="white-space:nowrap;"><strong>Statement II:</strong></span></td><td>At constant temperature, according to Boyle&rsquo;s law, volume is inversely proportional to the pressure.</td></tr></tbody></table>Choose the correct option from given ones:
<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td>1.</td><td><strong>Statement I</strong> is correct and <strong>Statement II</strong> is incorrect.</td></tr><tr><td>2.</td><td><strong>Statement I</strong> is incorrect and&nbsp;<strong>Statement II</strong> is correct.</td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td>Both <strong>Statement I</strong> and <strong>Statement II</strong> are correct.</td></tr><tr><td>4.</td><td>Both <strong>Statement I</strong> and <strong>Statement II</strong> are incorrect.</td></tr></tbody></table>

Given below are two statements:&nbsp;
<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td><span style="white-space:nowrap;"><strong>Assertion&nbsp;(A):</strong></span></td><td style="text-align: justify;">A real gas behaves like an ideal gas at low pressures and high temperatures.</td></tr><tr><td><span style="white-space:nowrap;"><strong>Reason&nbsp;(R):</strong></span></td><td style="text-align: justify;">At low pressures &amp; high temperatures, the intermolecular potential energy is much smaller than the kinetic energy of random motion of the molecules.</td></tr></tbody></table>
&nbsp;

<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td>1.</td><td style="text-align: justify;">Both&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;are True and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is the correct explanation of&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>2.</td><td style="text-align: justify;">Both&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;are True but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is not the correct explanation of&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td><strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;is True but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is False.</td></tr><tr><td>4.</td><td><strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;is False but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is True.</td></tr></tbody></table>

Given below are two statements:&nbsp;
<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td><span style="white-space:nowrap;"><strong>Assertion&nbsp;(A):</strong></span></td><td>The pressure exerted by a gas is due to the elastic collisions of the gas molecules with the walls of the container.</td></tr><tr><td><span style="white-space:nowrap;"><strong>Reason&nbsp;(R):</strong></span></td><td>When gas molecules undergo elastic collisions with the walls of the containing vessel, they rebound and exert a force. The net reaction force per unit area is the pressure.</td></tr></tbody></table>
&nbsp;

<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td>1.</td><td>Both&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;are True and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is the correct explanation of&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>2.</td><td>Both&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;are True but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is not the correct explanation of&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td><strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;is True but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is False.</td></tr><tr><td>4.</td><td><strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;is False but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is True.</td></tr></tbody></table>
